1
00:00:13,335 --> 00:00:22,140
[SPEAKER_00]: Bhante, I must confess, I tend to be rather lax when it comes to deep contemplation and reflection on life's intricacies.

2
00:00:23,201 --> 00:00:27,483
[SPEAKER_00]: However, I take solace in the fact that my idleness doesn't lead me astray.

3
00:00:28,524 --> 00:00:30,605
[SPEAKER_00]: I stand firmly in my virtuous path.

4
00:00:31,786 --> 00:00:38,990
[SPEAKER_00]: So, can I simply find contentment in this virtue, rest in my accomplishments, and trust that wisdom will come naturally with time?

5
00:00:42,552 --> 00:00:48,926
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's a bit of a broad of a question there, because there's so many other factors within that.

6
00:00:49,828 --> 00:00:51,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, the answer is both yes and no.

7
00:00:53,023 --> 00:01:12,587
[SPEAKER_01]: You could, if you remain virtuous, protecting that virtue, protecting the wholesome action, avoiding the unwholesome action, even if you don't necessarily are kind of exerting yourself in some effort to understand higher dhamma or more specific aspects of it, you could be progressing.

8
00:01:13,127 --> 00:01:20,208
[SPEAKER_01]: If on the other hand, like, you're virtuous, you're not breaking the precepts, but at the same time you're not watching intentions and subtler actions within that,

9
00:01:21,222 --> 00:01:33,769
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, not necessarily, you might... you might not be regressing as much, but at the same time, you're not abandoning the assumptions that you carry, the value of sensuality that you carry, the wrong views that you carry.

10
00:01:35,530 --> 00:01:37,591
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is no status quo, basically.

11
00:01:37,611 --> 00:01:43,074
[SPEAKER_01]: You're either developing forward, in terms of the right practice, or you are going backward, in terms of accumulating the wrong views.

12
00:01:45,175 --> 00:01:45,455
[SPEAKER_00]: Because

13
00:01:46,103 --> 00:01:49,505
[SPEAKER_00]: What you may... what this person might think is virtuous.

14
00:01:49,785 --> 00:01:50,325
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly.

15
00:01:50,345 --> 00:01:50,965
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

16
00:01:51,266 --> 00:02:04,052
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you might be slowing down your accumulation of wrong views through being virtuous and sense-restrained and careful, but you need to undo them, because you have not started in a neutral position.

17
00:02:04,712 --> 00:02:10,015
[SPEAKER_01]: You started by carrying all this burden of ignorance, avijjā and everything else upon it.

18
00:02:10,675 --> 00:02:11,956
[SPEAKER_01]: And that needs to be undone.

19
00:02:13,339 --> 00:02:17,541
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what they're really asking is, would that be undone if I just remain virtuous?

20
00:02:18,422 --> 00:02:19,042
[SPEAKER_01]: It could be.

21
00:02:19,182 --> 00:02:20,483
[SPEAKER_01]: It would take a lot longer.

22
00:02:22,324 --> 00:02:24,065
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you might run out of time, basically.

23
00:02:24,265 --> 00:02:26,366
[SPEAKER_01]: You might not be in this lifetime.

24
00:02:27,327 --> 00:02:28,667
[SPEAKER_01]: It would take a lot longer.

25
00:02:28,987 --> 00:02:34,650
[SPEAKER_01]: And then also, again, it's not just about, oh, I'm just keeping the precepts religiously, superstitiously.

26
00:02:35,831 --> 00:02:42,675
[SPEAKER_01]: But, well, does that imply you are also discerning your mind rooted in unwholesome intentions?

27
00:02:43,731 --> 00:02:44,752
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that becoming obvious?

28
00:02:44,773 --> 00:02:54,906
[SPEAKER_01]: Because many people can... many people do keep precepts, religious superstitiously, in the sense of, I will abstain from doing these things, and that's how devas and gods will protect me.

29
00:02:55,729 --> 00:02:58,331
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's my... that's the extent of my effort.

30
00:02:58,371 --> 00:03:03,176
[SPEAKER_01]: I just not do this, not because I know and see for myself that it's bad to do it.

31
00:03:03,636 --> 00:03:06,178
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just because I've been told to and I have...

32
00:03:06,198 --> 00:03:10,582
[SPEAKER_01]: I have that kind of belief that that will save me or it will eventually lead to my liberation.

33
00:03:10,642 --> 00:03:19,010
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like... well, in and of itself, that's just a nice and virtuous behavior that can, as the Buddha... as the suttas describe, maybe lead you to a heaven or something.

34
00:03:20,345 --> 00:03:25,088
[SPEAKER_01]: If you, if you kind of adhere to it in this lifetime, but that will run out eventually.

35
00:03:25,188 --> 00:03:25,348
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

36
00:03:25,368 --> 00:03:28,089
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like that faith doesn't necessarily develop wisdom.

37
00:03:28,869 --> 00:03:30,070
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, of course, no.

38
00:03:30,730 --> 00:03:47,298
[SPEAKER_01]: So, so it's like you can, you can just focus on the virtue, find contentment in it, but at the same time, okay, so now on top of it, yeah, I will not make any detailed effort and like really exerting myself in, in, in anxious pursuits of understanding the dhamma, but

39
00:03:48,028 --> 00:03:54,731
[SPEAKER_01]: When unwholesome things arise in my mind, although they're not against the precepts, you know, by body, am I entertaining them mentally?

40
00:03:54,811 --> 00:03:55,972
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I seeing the danger there?

41
00:03:56,052 --> 00:03:57,572
[SPEAKER_01]: Do I see where my intentions are rooted?

42
00:03:57,612 --> 00:03:58,313
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I avoiding it?

43
00:03:58,353 --> 00:03:59,213
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I cultivating it?

44
00:03:59,253 --> 00:04:00,013
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I enduring it?

45
00:04:00,874 --> 00:04:05,296
[SPEAKER_01]: If the answer to those things are no, then your virtue is kind of, well, sterilized.

46
00:04:07,197 --> 00:04:10,278
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just gonna stay there and you have some benefits and that's it.

47
00:04:12,308 --> 00:04:14,850
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still nicer than not being virtuous, but that's not what we're talking about.

48
00:04:14,910 --> 00:04:18,873
[SPEAKER_01]: We're talking about whether that can translate into development in terms of the dhamma.

49
00:04:19,393 --> 00:04:24,857
[SPEAKER_00]: And purified or completely accomplished in virtue would be being an arahant.

50
00:04:25,377 --> 00:04:26,658
[SPEAKER_00]: So, are you completely?

51
00:04:27,719 --> 00:04:28,119
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah.

52
00:04:28,179 --> 00:04:33,222
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, accomplished in virtue can mean being a sotāpanna.

53
00:04:34,003 --> 00:04:36,725
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, fully purifying that can lead to arahantship.

54
00:04:36,785 --> 00:04:39,707
[SPEAKER_01]: So, exactly like what you are

55
00:04:40,745 --> 00:04:46,190
[SPEAKER_01]: Finding contentment in... is it the virtue of a sotāpanna already?

56
00:04:46,270 --> 00:04:51,134
[SPEAKER_01]: Or it's clearly unsure, in which case, like, there will be a bit too risky to find contentment in that.

57
00:04:51,394 --> 00:04:53,836
[SPEAKER_01]: If only... to find contentment only in that.

58
00:04:54,597 --> 00:04:57,960
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you're still a putujana, you're not accomplished in virtue.

59
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:01,142
[SPEAKER_01]: Although you might not be breaking the precepts, you have not seen it for yourself.

60
00:05:01,783 --> 00:05:02,944
[SPEAKER_01]: Good is good, bad is bad.

61
00:05:03,244 --> 00:05:06,147
[SPEAKER_01]: Skillful is skillful, unskillful is unskillful.

62
00:05:06,977 --> 00:05:09,359
[SPEAKER_01]: You have not understood the root of suffering in the way out.

63
00:05:09,540 --> 00:05:12,282
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't see where the virtue is, correctly.

64
00:05:13,223 --> 00:05:14,164
[SPEAKER_01]: And that needs to be seen.

65
00:05:14,204 --> 00:05:19,910
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, if you want to remain contented with that, and not maybe make further effort, sure.

66
00:05:20,230 --> 00:05:23,953
[SPEAKER_01]: But even then, it's like... by that point, it will not be such a...

67
00:05:24,915 --> 00:05:36,783
[SPEAKER_01]: Tremendous task, because now you know what you need to do, so you would just basically be able to do it through your virtue, practicing withdrawal from unwholesome states, guarding of the sense doors, would be enough, actually.

68
00:05:36,823 --> 00:05:44,488
[SPEAKER_01]: You would be progressing in wisdom, if you have the right view.

69
00:05:44,708 --> 00:05:50,952
[SPEAKER_00]: And another thing that comes to mind is, it's nice to

70
00:05:54,263 --> 00:06:02,348
[SPEAKER_00]: Rest in your virtue, like to, you know, to experience the joy of being virtuous, of being blameless.

71
00:06:03,269 --> 00:06:03,849
[SPEAKER_00]: It's very nice.

72
00:06:04,690 --> 00:06:06,511
[SPEAKER_00]: But in a way, it's...

73
00:06:07,812 --> 00:06:10,614
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know if it's good to rest in it.

74
00:06:11,114 --> 00:06:11,674
[SPEAKER_00]: You know what I mean?

75
00:06:11,694 --> 00:06:18,699
[SPEAKER_00]: It's better to be working, striving further, than resting content.

76
00:06:18,799 --> 00:06:23,622
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the thing is, like, it's really more like resting in it does not require effort.

77
00:06:25,097 --> 00:06:32,202
[SPEAKER_01]: So, resting in virtue pretty much means, intentionally, making effort somewhere else.

78
00:06:35,184 --> 00:06:51,355
[SPEAKER_01]: So, and that's why I said what I said, like, does that mean, like, okay, I'm not making the effort towards the detailed understanding of the dhamma, but does that imply I'm not making effort to endure things on the mental level, to endure doubt, to endure hindrances, to not act out of them?

79
00:06:52,276 --> 00:06:53,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if he does, you're wrong.

80
00:06:54,044 --> 00:06:56,745
[SPEAKER_01]: Like that, that should not... that cannot be negotiated.

81
00:06:56,985 --> 00:06:57,766
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to do that.

82
00:06:57,846 --> 00:07:02,788
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether you're resting in virtue and simply a mere faith type, this is where the practice is.

83
00:07:02,988 --> 00:07:08,570
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, you don't have to be studying Pali, reading the suttas and pondering constantly on the teaching and so on.

84
00:07:09,231 --> 00:07:10,351
[SPEAKER_01]: That's someone's approach.

85
00:07:11,492 --> 00:07:18,835
[SPEAKER_01]: But you do have to recognize that if you're resting on the virtue, what else are you doing now?

86
00:07:18,855 --> 00:07:19,935
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, I'm resting on the virtue.

87
00:07:19,996 --> 00:07:23,117
[SPEAKER_01]: So, does that mean I'm tolerating unwholesome states?

88
00:07:24,038 --> 00:07:29,382
[SPEAKER_01]: Or am I enduring them rightly, without acting out, without accepting the mental value, delight in them?

89
00:07:31,264 --> 00:07:33,205
[SPEAKER_01]: Same with ill-will, same with distraction.

90
00:07:34,406 --> 00:07:35,727
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's where the practice is.

91
00:07:35,927 --> 00:07:37,789
[SPEAKER_01]: Virtue is a basis for this practice.

92
00:07:37,889 --> 00:07:45,895
[SPEAKER_01]: But if this practice is absent, you can have all the contentment you want in the basis, in the foundations, when there is no house on it.

93
00:07:46,396 --> 00:07:47,436
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot be safe there.

94
00:07:48,417 --> 00:07:51,159
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I... am I... am I...

95
00:07:52,479 --> 00:08:00,423
[SPEAKER_00]: Intentionally being content with the virtue that I have and intentionally now using that as an excuse not to practice further.

96
00:08:00,743 --> 00:08:01,484
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

97
00:08:01,504 --> 00:08:03,144
[SPEAKER_01]: You can become that, yeah.

98
00:08:03,364 --> 00:08:08,007
[SPEAKER_00]: I've done a certain amount of work and I must absorb the happiness here.

99
00:08:08,973 --> 00:08:11,234
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, I've got lots of work to do, but it's...

100
00:08:11,274 --> 00:08:14,475
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm justified in not doing any further work, because I have...

101
00:08:14,815 --> 00:08:22,499
[SPEAKER_01]: I am virtuous, and I must... That's... yeah, that's exactly the thing, because absorbing the happiness from it, and doing the work, are two separate things.

102
00:08:22,579 --> 00:08:23,799
[SPEAKER_01]: They're not mutually exclusive.

103
00:08:24,440 --> 00:08:29,462
[SPEAKER_01]: Being virtuous, resting in it, finding contentment in it, absorbing the happiness of it, it's effortless.

104
00:08:31,029 --> 00:08:35,371
[SPEAKER_01]: So, do you use that to avoid the effort that you would have been making on the right level?

105
00:08:35,411 --> 00:08:45,376
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, do you use that contentment and so on as a justification to not endure correctly the thoughts of sensuality, ill-will and distraction?

106
00:08:45,956 --> 00:08:53,420
[SPEAKER_01]: Or are you doing the work there and you just simply mean, yeah, I just don't want to have to be constantly thinking about themes from the suttas and so on?

107
00:08:53,440 --> 00:08:54,120
[SPEAKER_01]: In which case, fine.

108
00:08:54,360 --> 00:08:55,021
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to.

109
00:08:55,521 --> 00:08:57,502
[SPEAKER_01]: Keep your virtue and your things on the right level.

110
00:08:57,642 --> 00:08:58,582
[SPEAKER_01]: Discern the hindrances.

111
00:08:58,623 --> 00:08:59,463
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't act out of them.

112
00:09:00,432 --> 00:09:04,035
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't try to get rid of them, and find contentment in the virtue on account of that.

113
00:09:04,515 --> 00:09:04,855
[SPEAKER_01]: Fine.

114
00:09:05,015 --> 00:09:05,756
[SPEAKER_01]: Wisdom will develop.

115
00:09:06,276 --> 00:09:16,744
[SPEAKER_00]: In a way, it's like the right striving with becoming virtuous, the peace that comes from it, doesn't mean that you stop striving.

116
00:09:16,884 --> 00:09:17,344
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

117
00:09:17,384 --> 00:09:17,704
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

118
00:09:17,724 --> 00:09:19,346
[SPEAKER_00]: Striving becomes peaceful.

119
00:09:19,386 --> 00:09:20,987
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, striving becomes effortless as well.

120
00:09:21,067 --> 00:09:26,951
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you stop striving, to rest in peace, means you're not, you're not...

121
00:09:27,545 --> 00:09:30,027
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it means you are now striving towards something else.

122
00:09:30,467 --> 00:09:33,049
[SPEAKER_01]: You're striving towards the opposite of what you should be striving.

123
00:09:33,069 --> 00:09:41,975
[SPEAKER_01]: It means you're justifying your involvement with hindrances, sensuality, distraction, indolence, doubt, and so on.

124
00:09:45,518 --> 00:09:46,218
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's interesting, yeah.

125
00:09:46,238 --> 00:09:52,263
[SPEAKER_00]: I've heard people say, there's a time to rest and there's time to strive, to work.

126
00:09:53,678 --> 00:09:56,079
[SPEAKER_00]: Actually, if you're striving correctly... That is your resting.

127
00:09:56,099 --> 00:09:59,420
[SPEAKER_01]: That is your resting, so... And that's exactly what it said for an arahant.

128
00:10:00,080 --> 00:10:06,321
[SPEAKER_01]: Although he's beyond training, he remains in that training mode, because that is the most peaceful abiding.

129
00:10:06,341 --> 00:10:08,762
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not the same as... No, no, no, of course.

130
00:10:08,942 --> 00:10:11,103
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you can only understand that if you're an arahant.

131
00:10:14,744 --> 00:10:19,525
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, training mode requires effort, because

132
00:10:20,548 --> 00:10:25,371
[SPEAKER_01]: Your assumptions, your cravings, your being is still not extinguished and it's pulling you the opposite way.

133
00:10:26,092 --> 00:10:27,252
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the tension there.

134
00:10:27,352 --> 00:10:27,733
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not...

135
00:10:28,093 --> 00:10:30,314
[SPEAKER_01]: The tension is not caused by the training and restraint.

136
00:10:31,255 --> 00:10:36,578
[SPEAKER_01]: Training and restraint reveal the tension of the flow of asavas and sensuality and so on.

137
00:10:37,199 --> 00:10:46,745
[SPEAKER_01]: But obviously, unwise mind, that's not developed, associates the pain of that tension with restraint and with the training.

138
00:10:47,865 --> 00:10:53,086
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's the new criteria I introduced in my life, and look, now I suffer as a result of it.

139
00:10:53,467 --> 00:11:00,488
[SPEAKER_01]: Engaging with the training rightly, it's painful only because of the passions that are still not extinguished, and desires.

140
00:11:02,549 --> 00:11:09,151
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you extinguish the desires, cravings, the state of training is actually peaceful, abiding here and now.

141
00:11:10,891 --> 00:11:16,693
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why arahants remain in it, even if they're not exerting themselves in the actual practice of dhamma.

142
00:11:17,636 --> 00:11:22,105
[SPEAKER_01]: Mentally, they're entirely against that grain of old passion and everything else.

143
00:11:22,126 --> 00:11:23,609
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where they've been solidified in.

